// Full encyclopedia entries: Australia, south-australia. Built from the Vitis Mundi atlas plus researched rules and history; checked 14 November 2026.
(function(){
APXF["au-adelaide"] = {"lede": "A 'super zone' wrapping the Mount Lofty Ranges, Fleurieu and Barossa zones, created so that wines blended across the hills and plains near the city can carry a place name more specific than South Australia.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Grenache, Chardonnay"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It is used mainly for multi-regional blends; its value is that it guarantees the fruit is from the cooler, older vineyards near the capital rather than the irrigated Riverland.</p><p>Includes everything from Barossa's valley floor to the Adelaide Hills at 700 m and the coastal Fleurieu.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a 'super zone,' a GI that spans several zones or states, created for multi-regional blends.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The Adelaide super zone wraps the Mount Lofty Ranges, Fleurieu and Barossa zones so that wines blended across the hills and plains near the city can carry a single name.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Multi-regional South Australian blends.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Super zone: Mount Lofty Ranges + Fleurieu + Barossa.</p>"]], "producers": ["Penfolds", "Hardys"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-adelaide-hills"] = {"lede": "A long, narrow region of hills, orchards and stringybark forest running about 70 km along the Mount Lofty Ranges east of Adelaide.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1998"], ["Vineyard area", "About 3,831 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Shiraz, Pinot Gris, Grüner Veltliner, Nebbiolo"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Vineyards sit at roughly 300 to 700 m, which makes it one of the coolest regions in mainland South Australia, and it has become the state's centre for Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, cool-climate Shiraz and traditional-method sparkling.</p><p>German settlers planted vines at Hahndorf and Lobethal in the nineteenth century, but by the 1930s the Hills were orchard country. The modern era began with Brian Croser's Petaluma, whose Piccadilly Valley Chardonnay (from 1976 onward) and Croser sparkling proved the site, followed by Henschke at Lenswood, Stephen George's Ashton Hills (Pinot Noir) and Shaw + Smith, whose 1989 Sauvignon Blanc became a benchmark. In the 2000s and 2010s the Basket Range area became the heart of Australia's natural-wine movement, with Lucy Margaux, BK Wines, Ochota Barrels and Commune of Buttons making skin-contact whites, light reds and pét-nats.</p><p>About 1,710 growing degree days on Wine Australia's data, with 740 mm of annual rain, more than the Barossa or McLaren Vale. Soils are mostly ancient sandstones, schist, quartzite and siltstone, often weathered to grey-brown loam over clay. The northern end (Gumeracha, Birdwood) is warmer; the central Piccadilly Valley and Lenswood are the coolest and wettest.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Germans planted vines at Hahndorf in the 1840s, but the industry faded by the early twentieth century. Brian Croser founded Petaluma in 1976 and planted the Piccadilly Valley from 1979, showing the hills could make fine sparkling wine and Chardonnay; Shaw + Smith (1989) made Adelaide Hills Sauvignon Blanc famous. The GI was registered in 1998, with Lenswood and Piccadilly Valley as subregions.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Fresh Sauvignon Blanc, fine Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and cool-climate Shiraz; sparkling wine.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Altitude 400 to 700 m; Petaluma (Croser); Lenswood and Piccadilly Valley subregions.</p>"]], "producers": ["Petaluma", "Shaw + Smith", "Ashton Hills", "Tapanappa", "Henschke", "Ochota Barrels", "Lucy Margaux", "BK Wines", "Murdoch Hill", "Hahndorf Hill", "Deviation Road", "Bird in Hand", "Sidewood"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-adelaide-plains"] = {"lede": "Flat, hot market-garden country north of Adelaide around Virginia and Angle Vale, registered in 2002.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2002"], ["Vineyard area", "About 497 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Colombard, Nero d'Avola"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Much of the fruit goes into blends, but Primo Estate (Joe Grilli, who first made Colombard famous here and created the amarone-style Joseph Moda) and Ceravolo show the region can make characterful wine.</p><p>About 2,250 growing degree days and under 400 mm of annual rain; flat alluvial soils near Gulf St Vincent, irrigated.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Flat market-garden land north of Adelaide; Joe Grilli of Primo Estate showed it could make distinctive wines, including La Biondina Colombard. Registered 2002.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Warm reds; Colombard.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Hot plains; Primo Estate.</p>"]], "producers": ["Primo Estate", "Ceravolo", "Old Plains"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-barossa"] = {"lede": "The Barossa zone joins the warm valley floor of the Barossa Valley with the cooler, higher Eden Valley to the east.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1996"], ["Vineyard area", "About 13,519 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Riesling, Semillon"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Wines labelled simply 'Barossa' are usually blends across both, a historic practice: Barossa winemakers have long balanced the density of valley-floor Shiraz with the acidity and perfume of Eden Valley fruit. The two regions share a history of Silesian settlement, Lutheran churches and family growers whose names (Hoffmann, Kalleske, Lindner, Henschke) recur on labels and vineyard registers.</p><p>A single zone of about 1,690 square kilometres with 13,500 ha of vines, rising from about 110 m on the North Para River to over 600 m on the Barossa Range.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a zone, a large administrative division within a state; zones were drawn to cover the wine states completely, and most regions sit inside one.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The Barossa zone was drawn in 1996 to join the Barossa Valley and Eden Valley. Wines labelled simply 'Barossa' blend the two, a common practice for houses such as Penfolds, Henschke and Yalumba.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Barossa blends of valley Shiraz and Eden Valley fruit.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Zone: Barossa Valley + Eden Valley (+ High Eden).</p>"]], "producers": ["Penfolds", "Yalumba", "Henschke", "Seppeltsfield", "Torbreck"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-barossa-valley"] = {"lede": "The Barossa Valley is the heartland of Australian Shiraz and holds some of the oldest producing vines on earth.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1997"], ["Vineyard area", "About 11,156 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz (about half the vineyard), Cabernet Sauvignon, Grenache, Mourvèdre (Mataro), Semillon, Riesling"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>A shallow valley 25 km long, drained by the North Para River 60 km north-east of Adelaide, it was settled from 1842 by Lutheran farmers from Prussian Silesia, who called it 'Neu-Schlesien'. They planted mixed farms with a few rows of vines; when the fortified-wine boom came in the late nineteenth century the vines became the crop. Because South Australia kept phylloxera out, many of those own-rooted, dry-grown bush vines still crop: Langmeil's Freedom Shiraz (1843), Cirillo's Grenache at Light Pass (1848), Turkey Flat's Shiraz at Bethany, Hewitson's Old Garden Mourvèdre (1853) and Penfolds' Block 42 Cabernet at Kalimna (1888).</p><p>For decades this fruit went into fortified wine and then into anonymous blends; in the 1980s, with a government vine-pull scheme paying growers to grub up old vines, much was lost. Peter Lehmann, who guaranteed to buy growers' grapes during the crisis, Robert O'Callaghan at Rockford and Charlie Melton saved what remained, and in the 1990s American critics fell for the ripe, dense, chocolate-and-blackberry style. Since the 2010s the style has moved toward earlier picking, less new oak and more whole-bunch Grenache and Mataro, without losing the region's generosity.</p><p>The Barossa Old Vine Charter registers vines by age (Old 35+, Survivor 70+, Centenarian 100+, Ancestor 125+). The Barossa Grounds project grouped the valley's soils and climates into broad zones (commonly called the Northern Grounds around Moppa, Ebenezer and Greenock, which give the darkest, most tannic Shiraz; the Central Grounds around Marananga, Seppeltsfield and Tanunda; the warmer, sandier Southern Grounds around Rowland Flat and Lyndoch; and the cooler Eastern Edge and Western Ridge), which producers now cite on labels as informal sub-districts.</p><p>Warm and dry: Wine Australia records about 1,970 growing degree days and 209 mm of growing-season rain, so most of the oldest vines are dry-grown and rely on deep soils that hold winter rain. Soils change over short distances, from red-brown clay loams and ironstone in the north and west (Marananga, Greenock, Ebenezer) to deep sand over clay at Light Pass and Vine Vale and grey sandy loams in the south. Altitude runs from about 110 m on the valley floor to almost 600 m on the western ridge.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Silesian Lutherans fleeing religious persecution, led by Pastor August Kavel and financed by the merchant George Fife Angas, settled the Barossa from 1842 and planted vines alongside wheat and orchards. Johann Gramp planted at Jacob's Creek in 1847, the origin of the brand. Because South Australia stayed free of phylloxera, Barossa vines planted in the 1840s still produce, among the oldest Shiraz, Grenache and Mourvèdre in the world. In the 1980s the government paid growers to pull out old vines; Peter Lehmann, who bought growers' grapes when others would not, became a local hero. In 2009 the Barossa Old Vine Charter, developed by Yalumba and adopted regionally, classified vines as Old (35+ years), Survivor (70+), Centenarian (100+) and Ancestor (125+). The GI was registered in 1997.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Rich, ripe Shiraz with blackberry, chocolate and spice; Grenache and Mataro blends; Semillon; fortified wines.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Silesian Lutherans 1842; Jacob's Creek 1847; phylloxera-free old vines; Old Vine Charter 2009; Penfolds, Henschke, Torbreck.</p>"]], "producers": ["Penfolds", "Torbreck", "Rockford", "Langmeil", "Turkey Flat", "Seppeltsfield", "Yalumba", "Peter Lehmann", "Charles Melton", "Two Hands", "Kalleske", "John Duval", "Greenock Creek", "Hentley Farm", "Sami-Odi", "St Hallett", "Elderton", "Saltram"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-clare-valley"] = {"lede": "The Clare Valley, 120 km north of Adelaide, is not one valley but a series of narrow north-south valleys on a plateau between about 300 and 500 m, and it is Australia's most famous source of dry Riesling.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1999"], ["Vineyard area", "About 4,993 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Riesling, Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Grenache, Semillon, Malbec"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Clare Riesling is bone dry, smells of lime juice and lime blossom when young, and turns to toast, honey and kerosene over ten to twenty years. The region also makes structured Shiraz and Cabernet, from estates such as Wendouree and Jim Barry, that need years to soften.</p><p>Settlement was varied: the Austrian Jesuits founded Sevenhill in 1851 to make altar wine (they still do), Polish immigrants settled the Polish Hill River valley from 1856, and English and Cornish miners worked the copper mines at nearby Burra. Two sub-areas define Clare Riesling. Watervale, in the south, has red-brown loam over limestone and gives more floral, generous, earlier-drinking wines; Polish Hill, in a side valley to the east, has hard slate and shale and gives austere, tightly wound wines that need time.</p><p>Clare also changed how the world closes bottles. In August 2000, fourteen producers including Grosset, Mount Horrocks, Taylors, Leasingham, Mitchell and Knappstein announced they would bottle Riesling together under screwcap, with bottles from Saverglass and caps from Pechiney, because cork taint was ruining at least one bottle in twenty. It worked: aromatic Riesling ages slowly and predictably under screwcap, and within a decade most of Australia and New Zealand had switched.</p><p>Wine Australia records about 1,950 growing degree days, warmer than its reputation suggests, but cool nights, altitude and afternoon breezes preserve acidity. Soils are a patchwork: terra rossa and red loam over limestone at Watervale and Auburn, slate and shale at Polish Hill, sandy loam around Sevenhill and Penwortham.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Jesuits from Austria founded Sevenhill in 1851 to make altar wine, and it is still run by the Society of Jesus. Clare's Riesling, from slate and red soils, became a national benchmark. In 2000 a group of Clare winemakers led by Jeffrey Grosset bottled their Rieslings under screwcap together, defying the cork industry; within a decade most Australian and New Zealand wine had followed, one of the most influential decisions in modern wine.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Dry Riesling with lime, slate and floral notes, ageing to honey and toast; firm Shiraz and Cabernet.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Sevenhill Jesuits 1851; Riesling; 2000 screwcap initiative; Polish Hill and Watervale.</p>"]], "producers": ["Grosset", "Wendouree", "Jim Barry", "Kilikanoon", "Mount Horrocks", "Pikes", "Tim Adams", "Taylors (Wakefield)", "Sevenhill Cellars", "Skillogalee", "Leo Buring", "O'Leary Walker", "Mitchell", "Koerner"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-coonawarra"] = {"lede": "Coonawarra is Australia's most famous Cabernet Sauvignon region, and its fame rests on a strip of soil.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2003"], ["Vineyard area", "About 5,292 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon (more than half the vineyard), Shiraz, Merlot, Chardonnay"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>A narrow band of bright red terra rossa over soft white limestone, about 15 km long and rarely more than 2 km wide, runs north from Penola across flat country otherwise covered by black, poorly drained clays. Vines on the red strip ripen Cabernet with intense blackcurrant fruit, a mint and eucalyptus lift and firm but fine tannins.</p><p>The district was invented by John Riddoch, a Scottish pastoralist who in 1890 subdivided part of his property into small blocks for his Penola Fruit Colony and in 1891 built a stone winery with three gables. Bill Redman made the wine from 1901 and kept Coonawarra going through the lean decades; in 1951 the Wynn family bought Riddoch's winery and launched Wynns Coonawarra Estate, whose triple-gabled label is still the region's emblem. Mildara, Lindemans, Penfolds and others followed in the 1960s and 1970s.</p><p>Because the soil made the name, the boundary became a decade-long legal fight in the 1990s and early 2000s, as growers on black soil, those just outside and those on the terra rossa argued over what Coonawarra meant. The final boundary (registered in 2003) is broader than the red strip, so the best producers name the soil and the vineyard. The region's water table is high, so vines need little irrigation and suffer little from drought, but spring frost is a real risk.</p><p>Cool for its latitude: Wine Australia records about 1,570 growing degree days and a mean January temperature near 20.6 degrees C. Terra rossa (iron-rich red clay loam 20 to 50 cm deep) sits on calcrete and porous limestone; outside the strip are rendzina black soils and sandy flats. Altitude barely varies (about 50 to 130 m).</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>John Riddoch founded the Penola Fruit Colony in 1890 on a strip of terra rossa over limestone. Samuel and David Wynn bought the old Riddoch winery in 1951, and Wynns Coonawarra Estate built the region's name for Cabernet. Then came Australia's most bitter boundary fight. When the GI was proposed in the 1990s, growers argued for years over whether the line should follow the terra rossa strictly or include surrounding black and grey soils owned by some major companies. The case went from the Geographical Indications Committee to the Administrative Appeals Tribunal and the Federal Court before the final boundary was settled in 2003, a lesson in how much money a name can carry.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Cabernet Sauvignon with cassis, mint and eucalyptus, fine tannin and freshness; elegant Shiraz.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Terra rossa over limestone; Riddoch 1890; Wynns 1951; decade-long boundary dispute settled 2003.</p>"]], "producers": ["Wynns Coonawarra Estate", "Majella", "Balnaves", "Katnook", "Parker Coonawarra Estate", "Bowen Estate", "Zema", "Hollick", "Rymill", "Penley", "Leconfield", "Redman", "Lindeman's"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-currency-creek"] = {"lede": "A small region near Goolwa and the Murray Mouth, registered in 2001, with vineyards planted mostly since the 1990s.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2001"], ["Vineyard area", "About 1,053 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Its maritime climate and sandy soils give fruit-forward reds and fresh whites, much of it sold to larger wineries.</p><p>Close to Lake Alexandrina and the Southern Ocean; about 1,870 growing degree days and 460 mm of rain.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Named after a ship, the Currency Lass, the small region near the Murray Mouth was registered in 2001.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Soft reds and whites.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Murray Mouth; maritime.</p>"]], "producers": ["Ballast Stone", "Currency Creek Estate"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-eden-valley"] = {"lede": "Eden Valley is the high, cool eastern half of the Barossa: rolling granite and schist country at 400 to 600 m, with gum trees, sheep paddocks and widely spaced vineyards.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1997"], ["Vineyard area", "About 2,101 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Riesling, Shiraz, Chardonnay, Cabernet Sauvignon, Viognier"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It makes two of Australia's greatest wines. Its Riesling is fine, floral and lime-scented, more delicate than Clare's and capable of decades in bottle (Pewsey Vale, Heggies, Henschke Julius, Mesh). And its old Shiraz, above all Henschke's Hill of Grace and Mount Edelstone, shows that Australian Shiraz can be about perfume, spice and silk rather than weight.</p><p>Settlement began in the 1840s: Joseph Gilbert planted Pewsey Vale in 1847, and Johann Christian Henschke, a Silesian, started making wine at Keyneton in 1868. The Hill of Grace vineyard, named after the Gnadenberg (Hill of Grace) Lutheran church opposite, holds Shiraz planted in the 1860s; Cyril Henschke first bottled it alone with the 1958 vintage, a pioneering single-vineyard release. Yalumba revived Eden Valley Riesling and planted Viognier there in 1980, one of the first serious plantings in the New World.</p><p>About 1,800 growing degree days on Wine Australia's measure, but cold nights and wind make it feel cooler than the figure. Soils are thin and stony, derived from weathered granite, schist, ironstone and quartz, giving low yields. Altitude runs from about 220 m to over 630 m.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Joseph Gilbert planted Pewsey Vale in 1847, and the Hill of Grace vineyard at Keyneton, planted in the 1860s and farmed by the Henschke family, became one of the most famous single vineyards in Australia. Eden Valley's height and granite made it Riesling country: Yalumba revived Pewsey Vale in 1961 and Eden Valley Riesling became a benchmark. The GI was registered in 1997.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Taut, lime-scented Riesling that ages to toast; elegant, spicy Shiraz such as Hill of Grace.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>400 to 600 m; Riesling; Hill of Grace; Pewsey Vale 1847.</p>"]], "producers": ["Henschke", "Pewsey Vale", "Heggies", "Mountadam", "Eden Hall", "Mesh", "Flaxman", "Poonawatta", "Yalumba", "Torzi Matthews"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-far-north"] = {"lede": "A vast zone covering the north of South Australia, from the Flinders Ranges to the deserts, whose only region is the Southern Flinders Ranges.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1996"], ["Vineyard area", "About 176 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Riesling"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Almost all of its 858,000 square kilometres are desert with no vines.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a zone, a large administrative division within a state; zones were drawn to cover the wine states completely, and most regions sit inside one.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The Far North zone exists so that all of South Australia is covered; its only region is the Southern Flinders Ranges.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Warm reds.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Zone; Southern Flinders Ranges.</p>"]], "producers": [], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-fleurieu"] = {"lede": "The zone covering the Fleurieu Peninsula south of Adelaide and Kangaroo Island.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1996"], ["Vineyard area", "About 15,318 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Grenache, Chardonnay"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It includes McLaren Vale, Langhorne Creek, Currency Creek, Southern Fleurieu and Kangaroo Island, and together these crush around 15,000 hectares' worth of fruit, much of it red.</p><p>Strong maritime influence from Gulf St Vincent, the Southern Ocean and Lake Alexandrina.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a zone, a large administrative division within a state; zones were drawn to cover the wine states completely, and most regions sit inside one.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The Fleurieu zone takes its name from the peninsula, named by the French explorer Nicolas Baudin in 1802 after the French navy minister Charles Pierre Claret de Fleurieu.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Shiraz, Grenache, Cabernet from McLaren Vale and Langhorne Creek.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Zone: McLaren Vale, Langhorne Creek, Currency Creek, Southern Fleurieu, Kangaroo Island; named by Baudin.</p>"]], "producers": ["Hardys", "d'Arenberg", "Bleasdale"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-high-eden"] = {"lede": "The highest part of Eden Valley, registered as a subregion in 2001, around the Barossa Range above Springton and Flaxman Valley.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2001"], ["Vineyard area", "About 391 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Riesling, Chardonnay, Shiraz, Pinot Noir"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It was championed by David Wynn, who left the family's Wynns business to plant Mountadam here in 1972 and made one of Australia's early cool-climate Chardonnays. Riesling is tight and slow to open; Chardonnay and sparkling base are crisp.</p><p>Altitudes up to about 630 m, on quartzite, schist and sandy soils, cooler and windier than the rest of Eden Valley.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a subregion, which must meet the same tests as a region, at least five vineyards of five hectares and 500 tonnes, and be substantially discrete within its parent region.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The highest part of Eden Valley became a subregion in 2001, one of the earliest in Australia, defined by elevation along the Barossa Range.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Fine, mineral Riesling and Chardonnay; cool Shiraz.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Subregion of Eden Valley (2001); highest altitude.</p>"]], "producers": ["Mountadam", "Pewsey Vale", "Eden Hall"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-kangaroo-island"] = {"lede": "Australia's third-largest island, 13 km off the Fleurieu Peninsula, has about 144 ha of vines spread among small family producers.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2000"], ["Vineyard area", "About 144 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>The bushfires of December 2019 and January 2020 burnt about half the island and damaged several vineyards.</p><p>Cool and maritime (about 1,625 growing degree days), with laterite and ironstone soils.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The island's small family producers lost vineyards and cellars in the bushfires of December 2019 and January 2020, which burned nearly half the island.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Bordeaux blends, Shiraz, Sauvignon Blanc.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Island; 2019 to 2020 fires.</p>"]], "producers": ["The Islander Estate", "Dudley Wines", "False Cape"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-langhorne-creek"] = {"lede": "A flat, quiet region on the floodplains of the Bremer and Angas rivers near Lake Alexandrina, and one of Australia's great unsung sources of soft, fleshy red.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1998"], ["Vineyard area", "About 6,093 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, Merlot, Malbec, Chardonnay"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It has about 6,100 ha of vines, more than McLaren Vale's area of land would suggest, and much of its fruit disappears into famous blends: Wolf Blass's Black Label and many big-company Cabernet Shiraz blends rely on it.</p><p>Frank Potts founded Bleasdale here in 1850 and the family still runs it; Potts cut red gum from the riverbanks to build a huge lever press that still stands. Growers historically flooded their vineyards in winter by diverting the rivers, and the 'Lake Doctor', an afternoon breeze off Lake Alexandrina, cools ripening fruit. Saltram's Metala vineyard dates from 1891.</p><p>Low-lying (under 65 m), with deep alluvial sandy loams over clay. About 1,970 growing degree days but only 389 mm of annual rain, so water from the lake and rivers matters.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Frank Potts founded Bleasdale in 1850 and built a huge red-gum lever press that still survives. The river flats, flooded each winter from the Bremer and Angas, grow soft, generous reds that big companies such as Wolf Blass long used in blends.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Soft, plush Cabernet and Shiraz; Malbec.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Bleasdale 1850; flood irrigation; blending fruit.</p>"]], "producers": ["Bleasdale", "Bremerton", "Lake Breeze", "Heartland", "Saltram (Metala)", "Wolf Blass", "Kimbolton"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-lenswood"] = {"lede": "A high, apple-growing valley in the central Adelaide Hills, registered in 1998 as one of Australia's first subregions.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1998"], ["Vineyard area", "About 245 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Henschke's Lenswood vineyard (Croft Chardonnay, Giles Pinot Noir), Knappstein Lenswood and Pike & Joyce showed its Chardonnay and Pinot have the acidity for long ageing.</p><p>Steep slopes at roughly 400 to 560 m, on podzolic soils over sandstone and schist; cooler and wetter than most of the Hills.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a subregion, which must meet the same tests as a region, at least five vineyards of five hectares and 500 tonnes, and be substantially discrete within its parent region.</p>"], ["History", "<p>One of Australia's first subregions (1998), Lenswood was apple country before Henschke, Knappstein and others planted vines.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Early subregion 1998; Henschke Lenswood.</p>"]], "producers": ["Henschke", "Pike & Joyce", "Anvers", "Geoff Weaver"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-limestone-coast"] = {"lede": "The flat south-east corner of South Australia, a former seabed of limestone ridges and swamps that runs from Padthaway to Mount Gambier.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1996"], ["Vineyard area", "About 15,405 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, Merlot, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Its regions (Coonawarra, Wrattonbully, Padthaway, Mount Benson, Robe and Mount Gambier) share cool maritime air and soils of terra rossa or sand over limestone, and together grow about 15,400 ha of vines.</p><p>Low relief, high water tables and a cool climate moderated by the Southern Ocean.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a zone, a large administrative division within a state; zones were drawn to cover the wine states completely, and most regions sit inside one.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The Limestone Coast zone covers South Australia's south-east, a former seabed. Wineries here, from Coonawarra to Robe, share a cool maritime climate and limestone.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, cool-climate whites.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Zone: Coonawarra, Padthaway, Wrattonbully, Mount Benson, Robe, Mount Gambier.</p>"]], "producers": ["Wynns Coonawarra Estate", "Majella", "Tapanappa"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-lower-murray"] = {"lede": "The zone covering South Australia's stretch of the Murray River, whose only region is the Riverland.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1996"], ["Vineyard area", "About 22,261 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Merlot"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It is the largest wine-producing zone in the state by volume. In 2026 the Riverland alone crushed about 389,000 tonnes, close to a third of Australia's harvest, so the zone's fortunes are a barometer of the bulk-wine market.</p><p>Hot, dry and irrigated from the Murray.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a zone, a large administrative division within a state; zones were drawn to cover the wine states completely, and most regions sit inside one.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The Lower Murray zone covers South Australia's stretch of the Murray River, irrigated since the Chaffey brothers founded Renmark in 1887.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Inexpensive, high-volume wine.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Zone; only region Riverland.</p>"]], "producers": ["Angove", "Accolade"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-mclaren-vale"] = {"lede": "McLaren Vale is a Mediterranean pocket between the Mount Lofty Ranges and Gulf St Vincent, 40 minutes south of Adelaide, and it makes some of Australia's most interesting warm-climate reds.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1997"], ["Vineyard area", "About 7,172 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Grenache, Cabernet Sauvignon, Mourvèdre, Fiano, Nero d'Avola, Vermentino, Tempranillo"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It was planted almost as soon as South Australia was settled: John Reynell put in vines at Reynella in 1838, and Thomas Hardy, who bought Tintara in 1876, built the business that became the country's biggest wine company. Old dry-grown Shiraz and Grenache survive across the region, many over 80 years old.</p><p>The style of the region changed after 2000. Shiraz remains the main grape, but Grenache from the deep sands of Blewitt Springs, picked earlier and made with whole bunches, has become its signature: pale, perfumed and fine, from producers such as Yangarra, SC Pannell, Bekkers, Ox Hardy and Clarendon Hills. Hot, dry summers and limited water have pushed growers toward Mediterranean varieties: Coriole was among the first to plant Sangiovese and Fiano, and Fiano, Vermentino, Nero d'Avola, Montepulciano and Tempranillo are spreading. The region also leads Australia in organic and biodynamic certification.</p><p>McLaren Vale's growers mapped their geology in 2010 and found around 40 rock and soil units, ranging from recent sands to rocks more than 500 million years old. The region now uses an informal district map with names such as Blewitt Springs, Clarendon, Kangarilla, McLaren Flat, Seaview, Willunga and Sellicks Foothills, so drinkers can tell a sandy, perfumed Blewitt Springs Grenache from a dense, ironstone-grown Seaview Shiraz.</p><p>About 1,940 growing degree days and only 211 mm of growing-season rain. Afternoon sea breezes off Gulf St Vincent moderate the heat. Soils vary sharply: deep, white-grey sands over clay at Blewitt Springs, ironstone and red clay at Seaview, terra rossa and limestone in the south around Willunga, and older siltstones and quartzites near the hills at Clarendon (up to about 400 m).</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>John Reynell planted vines at Reynella in 1838, and Thomas Hardy, who started at Bankside near Adelaide in 1853, bought Tintara here in 1876. d'Arenberg was founded by Joseph Osborn in 1912. Mediterranean climate and old bush vines made McLaren Vale a source of Grenache and Shiraz for blending, until a generation of growers in the 1990s and 2000s promoted its own identity. In 2009 the Scarce Earth project, based on a geological map of the region's 40-plus soil types, began recognising single-site Shiraz, and the region became a leader in sustainable and organic farming.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Rich, warm Shiraz and Grenache with dark fruit, chocolate and Mediterranean herbs; Mataro; Fiano and other Mediterranean varieties.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Reynell 1838; Hardy 1853; d'Arenberg; Grenache; Scarce Earth; Gulf St Vincent breezes.</p>"]], "producers": ["d'Arenberg", "Yangarra", "SC Pannell", "Wirra Wirra", "Coriole", "Clarendon Hills", "Hardys", "Kay Brothers", "Chapel Hill", "Samuel's Gorge", "Bekkers", "Ox Hardy", "Hither & Yon", "Mitolo", "Paxton", "Gemtree", "Brash Higgins", "Ministry of Clouds"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-mount-benson"] = {"lede": "A coastal region near Kingston SE, planted from the late 1980s, notable for investment from the Rhône: Michel Chapoutier planted biodynamic Shiraz here in the late 1990s.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1997"], ["Vineyard area", "About 534 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>The climate is cool and windy, giving peppery Shiraz.</p><p>Terra rossa and sand over limestone within sight of the ocean; about 1,520 growing degree days.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Planted from the late 1980s, Mount Benson drew Michel Chapoutier from the Rhône, who planted biodynamic Shiraz here in the late 1990s.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Elegant Shiraz and Sauvignon Blanc.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Chapoutier; maritime; limestone.</p>"]], "producers": ["M. Chapoutier Australia", "Cape Jaffa", "Norfolk Rise"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-mount-gambier"] = {"lede": "South Australia's coolest region, around the extinct volcano and Blue Lake at Mount Gambier, registered in 2010.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2010"], ["Vineyard area", "About 273 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Riesling"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Small vineyards make crisp whites, Pinot Noir and sparkling base.</p><p>About 1,450 growing degree days, volcanic and limestone soils, frequent cloud and wind.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Registered in 2010, South Australia's coolest region sits around the extinct volcano and its Blue Lake.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Crisp whites, Pinot Noir, sparkling.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Coolest SA region; volcano.</p>"]], "producers": ["Herbert Vineyard", "Coola Road"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-mount-lofty-ranges"] = {"lede": "The zone that follows the Mount Lofty Ranges north and south of Adelaide, grouping three very different regions: the cool Adelaide Hills, the warm Clare Valley 100 km north, and the flat Adelaide Plains.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1996"], ["Vineyard area", "About 9,541 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Riesling, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It is rarely seen on labels; its regions do the work.</p><p>Elevations rise to over 700 m in the Adelaide Hills; the ranges catch more rain than the plains and the Barossa.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a zone, a large administrative division within a state; zones were drawn to cover the wine states completely, and most regions sit inside one.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The Mount Lofty Ranges zone groups three very different regions that share the hills around Adelaide: the cool Adelaide Hills, the warm Clare Valley and the flat Adelaide Plains.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Varied.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Zone: Adelaide Hills, Clare Valley, Adelaide Plains.</p>"]], "producers": ["Petaluma", "Grosset", "Shaw + Smith"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-padthaway"] = {"lede": "Planted from the 1960s by Seppelt and Lindemans, who were looking for cheap land with good water and a cool climate, Padthaway (originally called Keppoch) became a major source of Chardonnay for national brands; Hardys' Eileen Hardy Chardonnay long relied on it.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1999"], ["Vineyard area", "About 4,066 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Chardonnay, Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Today it supplies Shiraz and Cabernet too, with a few local labels.</p><p>Terra rossa and sandy loams over limestone, on a low ridge; about 1,700 growing degree days and 480 mm of rain, warmer than Coonawarra.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Seppelt and Lindemans planted here from the 1960s looking for cheap land, good water and a cool climate, and Padthaway became a key source of white wine for their brands. The area was long called Keppoch; the name Padthaway, after the homestead, was adopted for the GI to avoid confusion.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Chardonnay, Shiraz, sparkling base.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Seppelt and Lindemans 1960s; formerly Keppoch.</p>"]], "producers": ["Henry's Drive", "Browns of Padthaway", "Padthaway Estate"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-piccadilly-valley"] = {"lede": "A small, cool valley beneath Mount Lofty, registered in 2000.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2000"], ["Vineyard area", "About 221 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Brian Croser planted Petaluma's Tiers vineyard here in 1979, and the Tiers Chardonnay (now under Croser's Tapanappa label) is one of Australia's most precise. It is also prime country for sparkling base wine.</p><p>Among the highest and wettest vineyards in the Hills, at about 500 to 700 m, on brown loam over clay and sandstone.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a subregion, which must meet the same tests as a region, at least five vineyards of five hectares and 500 tonnes, and be substantially discrete within its parent region.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Brian Croser planted Petaluma's Tiers vineyard here in 1979; Tiers Chardonnay became one of Australia's finest. Registered 2000.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Fine Chardonnay and sparkling base.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Subregion; Tiers vineyard 1979.</p>"]], "producers": ["Tapanappa", "Petaluma", "Nepenthe"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-riverland"] = {"lede": "The Riverland, along the Murray around Renmark, Berri, Loxton and Waikerie, is Australia's largest wine region by tonnage, with about 21,800 ha of vines.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1998"], ["Vineyard area", "About 21,816 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Merlot, Colombard, Petit Verdot, Nero d'Avola, Montepulciano, Fiano"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>In 2026 it crushed about 389,000 tonnes, roughly 30 percent of the national total, even in a short year. The Canadian Chaffey brothers began irrigation at Renmark in 1887, and soldier-settler blocks after both world wars filled it with family growers. Its fruit makes cask, bulk and branded wine for the world.</p><p>The Riverland is also where Australia is rethinking warm-climate wine. Growers such as Ashley Ratcliff (Ricca Terra), 919 Wines, Bassham and Delinquente have planted Nero d'Avola, Montepulciano, Fiano, Vermentino and Touriga Nacional, varieties that hold acidity in the heat and need less water. The falling demand for cheap red since 2020 has hit the region hardest: many growers have left grapes unpicked or pulled out vines.</p><p>Hot and dry: about 2,350 growing degree days, 148 mm of growing-season rain and 254 mm a year. Every vine depends on Murray water allocations. Sandy loams over limestone (mallee country).</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The Chaffey brothers, Canadian irrigation engineers, founded Renmark in 1887. The Riverland became Australia's largest wine region by tonnage, supplying cask and branded wine.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Inexpensive Shiraz, Chardonnay and blends; some alternative varieties.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Largest region by tonnage; Murray irrigation; Chaffey brothers 1887.</p>"]], "producers": ["Angove", "Banrock Station", "Berri Estates", "Ricca Terra", "919 Wines", "Delinquente", "Bassham"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-robe"] = {"lede": "A cool coastal region around the fishing town of Robe, registered in 2006.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2006"], ["Vineyard area", "About 677 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Large vineyards supply fruit to Treasury Wine Estates and others; local sparkling and Pinot show the climate.</p><p>One of the coolest Limestone Coast regions (about 1,485 growing degree days), on limestone ridges and sand.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>A historic port turned fishing town, Robe was planted with large vineyards from the 1990s; registered 2006.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Cool-climate Shiraz, Chardonnay.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Coastal; registered 2006.</p>"]], "producers": ["Woodsoak", "Karatta"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-south-australia"] = {"lede": "The state GI covers every vineyard in South Australia and appears on blends drawn from several regions, such as multi-regional Shiraz from the big companies.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Grenache, Riesling, Merlot"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>South Australia crushes roughly half of Australia's grapes, and as a phylloxera-free state with tight quarantine on vine material it holds most of the country's pre-1900 plantings.</p><p>From the hot, irrigated Riverland to the cool Limestone Coast and the elevated Mount Lofty Ranges; the common thread is winter rainfall and dry summers.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a state or territory GI, the top level of the hierarchy (state, zone, region, subregion).</p>"], ["History", "<p>South Australia crushes roughly half of Australia's grapes and was never hit by phylloxera, thanks to strict quarantine since the 1890s; that is why it holds some of the oldest Shiraz, Grenache and Mourvèdre vines in the world. Germans fleeing religious persecution, English settlers and Cornish miners all shaped its wine culture.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Multi-regional Shiraz and blends.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>About half the national crush; phylloxera-free; old vines.</p>"]], "producers": ["Penfolds", "Yalumba", "Hardys", "Jacob's Creek"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-southern-fleurieu"] = {"lede": "The hilly tip of the Fleurieu Peninsula, from Mount Compass to Cape Jervis and Victor Harbor, registered in 2001.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2001"], ["Vineyard area", "About 487 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Cooler and wetter than McLaren Vale next door, it suits Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay and medium-bodied reds.</p><p>About 1,640 growing degree days and 700 mm of annual rain, with Southern Ocean winds.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The hilly tip of the peninsula was registered in 2001; cool, wet and windy compared with McLaren Vale.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Cool-climate reds and whites.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Cooler than McLaren Vale.</p>"]], "producers": ["Salomon Estate", "Mosquito Hill"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-southern-flinders-ranges"] = {"lede": "Small vineyards in the hills around Wirrabara, Port Pirie and Laura, registered in 2003, mostly making full, warm-climate reds and some Riesling for local producers.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2003"], ["Vineyard area", "About 175 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Riesling, Grenache"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It is the northernmost wine region in South Australia, and its cooler hill sites near Wirrabara and Bundaleer Forest show that altitude can offset the heat of the plains below.</p><p>Warm (about 2,220 growing degree days) but with cooler pockets at altitude up to about 950 m.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Registered in 2003 for small vineyards around Wirrabara and Laura.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Warm reds and Riesling.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Northern SA hills.</p>"]], "producers": ["Bundaleer Wines", "Belalie Bend"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-the-peninsulas"] = {"lede": "A zone covering the Eyre and Yorke peninsulas west of Adelaide, with about 70 ha of scattered vineyards, notably around Boston Bay near Port Lincoln and on Yorke Peninsula.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "1996"], ["Vineyard area", "About 72 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It has no registered regions.</p><p>Maritime, on limestone and sand.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a zone, a large administrative division within a state; zones were drawn to cover the wine states completely, and most regions sit inside one.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The Peninsulas zone covers the Eyre and Yorke peninsulas, with a few scattered vineyards.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Small-scale reds and whites.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Zone; Eyre and Yorke peninsulas.</p>"]], "producers": ["Boston Bay Wines", "Barley Stacks"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["au-wrattonbully"] = {"lede": "Wrattonbully sits just north of Coonawarra and shares its terra rossa over limestone, but its land is more undulating and less prone to frost, and it ripens grapes a little earlier.", "facts": [["Category", "GI"], ["Recognized", "2005"], ["Vineyard area", "About 2,666 ha"], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Tempranillo"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Most vineyards date from the 1990s, many owned by big companies whose fruit goes into Coonawarra-style blends. Brian Croser's Tapanappa makes its Whalebone Vineyard Cabernet Shiraz here from vines planted in 1974, named for a 35-million-year-old whale skeleton found in a cave beneath it.</p><p>About 1,650 growing degree days; terra rossa and sandy loam over limestone, at 75 to 150 m, with caves and sinkholes in the karst below.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>Australia's GI system was created in 1993, when amendments to the Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation Act set up the Geographical Indications Committee, partly to meet the terms of the 1994 wine agreement with the European Community, under which Australia also began phasing out names such as Burgundy, Champagne and Port. The committee, now part of Wine Australia, determines boundaries on application from local growers, following criteria that include the area's history, name, geology, climate and the existence of a viticultural identity. A GI on a label requires at least 85% of the wine from that GI, and the same 85% applies to vintage and variety. There are no rules on grapes, yields or winemaking: an Australian GI defines a place, not a style. This is a region, which under the regulations must be a single tract of land with at least five independently owned vineyards of at least five hectares each and usually produce at least 500 tonnes of grapes a year, and must be measurably homogeneous in its grape-growing attributes.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Growers first proposed the name Koppamurra, but objections from a winery that used it as a brand led to a fight; the region was registered in 2005 under the older local name Wrattonbully, from an Aboriginal word.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Cabernet, Shiraz, Merlot from terra rossa.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>North of Coonawarra; terra rossa; Koppamurra name dispute.</p>"]], "producers": ["Tapanappa", "Terre à Terre", "Smith & Hooper", "Ruckus Estate", "Yalumba"], "sources": [["Wine Australia: Register of protected GIs and other terms", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/register-of-protected-gis-and-other-terms"], ["Wine Australia: Geographical indications", "https://www.wineaustralia.com/labelling/geographical-indications"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
})();
